---
title: Pressure Cooker Garlicky Cuban Pork
description: Pork shoulder marinated in grapefruit, lime, and garlic, then pressure-cooked until it shreds.
tags: [pressure-cooker]
course: main
icons: [garlic, meat, pot-food]
cuisine: Cuban
source:
  name: NYT Cooking
  author: Melissa Clark
  url: https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/1018851-pressure-cooker-garlicky-cuban-pork
servings: 8
time: 3h30m
---

= Marinate

In a #blender{} or mini food processor, blend the @garlic cloves{8}, the juice of the @grapefruit{1}(about 2/3 cup of juice), the zest and juice of the @lime{1}(zest finely grated), @extra-virgin olive oil{2%tbsp}, @light brown sugar{2%tbsp}, @fresh oregano leaves{1%tbsp}, @ground cumin{2%tsp}, and @kosher salt{1 1/2%tbsp}(plus more to taste) until smooth.

Pour the marinade into a #large bowl{}, add the @boneless pork shoulder{4-5%lb}(cut into 4 pieces) and @bay leaf{1}, and toss to coat. Cover and marinate at room temperature for ~marinate{1%hour}.

> **Making Ahead** — The pork can marinate in the refrigerator for up to 6 hours instead.

= Cook

Heat the remaining @&extra-virgin olive oil{1%tbsp} in the #pressure cooker{} on its sauté setting, set to high if it has one. Lift the pork out of the marinade, shaking off the excess, and keep the marinade. Working in batches, brown the pork on all sides, about ~brown{12%minutes}, moving the browned pieces to a plate as you go.

> **Browning in a Skillet** — If your pressure cooker can't sauté, brown the pork in a large skillet. Afterwards add 1 tablespoon of water to the skillet, scrape up the browned bits with a wooden spoon, and add them to the pot.

Return all the pork to the pot with any juices from the plate, then pour in the reserved marinade. Lock the lid and cook on high pressure for ~pressure{80%minutes}. Let the pressure release naturally.

= Finish

Lift the pork out of the cooking liquid. Taste the liquid, and if it seems bland or thin, boil it down on the sauté setting until it thickens slightly and tastes stronger, ~reduce{7-15%minutes}. Remove the bay leaf and add a little salt if it needs it.

> **Skimming the Fat** — Pour the liquid through a fat separator, or let it settle and spoon the fat off the top.

Shred the pork with your hands or two forks. Toss it with a generous amount of the cooking liquid, 1 1/2 to 2 cups, and serve with @cilantro{}(chopped), @lime wedges{}, and @hot sauce{}, plus @?tortillas{} and @?fresh tomato salsa{} if you like.
